\section{Does Any of This Hold for Regression?}
\label{sec:reg}

Everything above is classification, scored by AUC. That is one loss, on
one kind of task, and a result that only holds there is worth less than it
looks. So we ran the whole study again on regression.

The learner does not change. A {\tt balsa} leaf already holds the $y$
column as a {\tt Num}, and {\tt mid} already returns its mean
(Listing~\ref{lst:data}), so the same @@loc@@ lines that predicted a class
predict a number. What changes is the rig around it: the score, and the
corpus.

\subsection{The Score}

Regression targets carry their own units, so a mean absolute error of 3
means nothing until you know whether $y$ is a price or a probability.
Averaging raw MAE over a corpus just reports which data sets have the
bigger numbers. We therefore divide by the error of the laziest learner
there is:
\[ \mathit{rel} = \mathrm{MAE} \;/\; \mathrm{MAE}(\text{always predict the
training mean}) \]
so $\mathit{rel}=1$ is no better than that lazy learner, $\mathit{rel}=0$
is perfect, and {\bf lower is better} --- the reverse of every AUC figure
above. The divisor is a denominator, not a treatment: its own
$\mathit{rel}$ is 1.0 on every fold, a constant column that would flatter
any variance decomposition it was included in.

This is not a neutral choice. The mean minimises {\em squared} error,
while MAE asks for the {\em median}, so the denominator is slightly
handicapped. We keep it because it is the comparison a reader makes
anyway ({\em is this model worth more than knowing nothing?}) and because
it is the one baseline that needs no tuning, no seed and no code.

\subsection{The Corpus}

@@nAll@@ data sets: @@nA@@ from {\tt moot/regression}, and @@nB@@ SE sets
from {\tt moot/optimize} ({\tt config}, {\tt process} and {\tt systems}).
Where a set offers several goals we predict the first, in file order, and
the others are neither inputs nor scored. Folds, seeds and rows are the
5x2 of Figures~\ref{fig:se} and~\ref{fig:nose}, replayed from the recorded
test row ids, so all four treatments see exactly the same splits.

What we left out, and why, because an exclusion no one states is an
exclusion no one can check:

\begin{itemize}
\item {\tt hungarian} (2 distinct $y$ values) and {\tt cleveland} (5) are
classification sets wearing a numeric column. {\tt quake} has a
coefficient of variation of 0.03: its $y$ is nearly constant, so the
denominator above is nearly zero and $\mathit{rel}$ is nearly meaningless.
\item {\tt delta.ailerons} and {\tt delta.elevators} have a
comma-delimited header over space-delimited rows, which no reader in this
study parses.
\item {\tt optimize/hpo} is 35 copies of one hyper-parameter sweep;
{\tt optimize/health\_data} holds a binary goal.
\item {\tt optimize/binary\_config} is dropped for a reason worth
reporting, since it is about our learner and not about the data. Its sets
are synthetic SAT instances whose columns are nearly all constant: 1030 of
the 1044 $x$ columns in {\tt FFM-1000-200-0.50-SAT-1} hold a single value,
and 106 of 124 in {\tt Scrum10k}. With {\tt the.rf} sampling
$\sqrt{|x|}$ columns at each node, most nodes there draw nothing they can
split on, and the group measures our column sampler rather than any
learner. LightGBM, which drops zero-variance features for free,
scored @@bcx@@ times better there than {\tt balsa(30)} (mean
$\mathit{rel}$ @@bc_lgbm@@ against @@bc_b30@@). A corpus can be a trap.
\end{itemize}

TabICL is capped by rows. It is an in-context learner, so every training
row enters the transformer's context: one fold costs about
$3.3{\times}10^{-4} (\mathit{rows}/2)^{1.4}$ seconds and
$0.5 + 2.1(\mathit{rows}/2)/5500$ GB, which for {\tt javagc} alone would
ask 32GB for a single fold. At a cap of 20,000 rows it finishes
@@nRan@@ of the @@nAll@@ sets in about two hours under 9GB. The other
@@nSkip@@ are @@skip@@. Table~\ref{tab:regfolds} is therefore built over the
@@nRan@@ sets TabICL finished, with one extra row for the other three
treatments over everything.

