\subsection{What We Found}

\paragraph{The ordering survives, and TabICL still leads.} Mean
$\mathit{rel}$ over all @@nAll@@ sets is @@all_b1@@ for a single tree,
@@mu_lgbm@@ for LightGBM and @@all_b30@@ for {\tt balsa(30)}; TabICL, on
the @@nRan@@ sets under its row cap, is @@mu_tab@@. The ranked-best
counts in Table~\ref{tab:regfolds} say it by counting, over those same
@@nRan@@ sets: TabICL first on @@w_tab@@, LightGBM on @@w_lgbm@@,
{\tt balsa(30)} on @@w_b30@@, the single tree on @@w_b1@@.

So one of \S\ref{sec:tabicl5x2}'s two claims travels and one does not.
TabICL leading every group under a different loss on a different corpus
is the stronger result, and we did not expect it to be this clean. But
the tie we reported between {\tt balsa(30)} and LightGBM does not
survive: in classification {\tt balsa(30)} was ranked best on 14 sets
against LightGBM's 9, and here the count runs the other way,
@@w_lgbm@@ against @@w_b30@@, with LightGBM ahead on mean
$\mathit{rel}$ as well. We report that rather than bury it. The next
paragraphs are about where the reversal comes from, and the short answer
is that it is about size, and about a cap in our own configuration.

\paragraph{But the cost gap does too.} Summed over folds, one thread
each: {\tt balsa(30)} @@sec_b30@@s, LightGBM @@sec_lgbm@@s, TabICL
@@sec_tab@@s --- @@costx@@ times LightGBM, and that excludes the
@@nSkip@@ sets it could not be given at all. Table~\ref{tab:baseline}'s
objection stands: a baseline must be cheap to apply.

\paragraph{Size decides between the other two.} On the @@nBig@@ sets of
1000 rows or more, LightGBM averages @@big_lgbm@@ against {\tt
balsa(30)}'s @@big_b30@@ and is ranked best on @@wbig_lgbm@@ of them
against @@wbig_b30@@. On the @@nSml@@ smaller sets the order reverses:
@@sml_lgbm@@ against @@sml_b30@@, @@wsml_lgbm@@ ranked best against
@@wsml_b30@@. The totals in Table~\ref{tab:regfolds} average two opposite
regimes, and anyone quoting only the total is quoting a number that
describes neither. LightGBM also fails to beat the lazy learner outright
on @@flat@@ of the @@nAll@@ sets; their median size is @@flatmed@@ rows,
against @@restmed@@ for the rest.

\paragraph{The ensemble pays, once it is fed.} An earlier draft of
this section reported a puzzle: the one-to-thirty step barely moved
the needle, and on the {\tt config} sets the single tree {\em won}
outright. Bagging is supposed to be a variance killer; ours was a
rounding error that sometimes ran backwards. Two suspects, both ours
rather than the method's: {\tt the.cap} trimmed training to @@cap@@
rows before any tree was built (each tree then saw half of those, so
thirty @@caphalf@@-row trees were asked to beat one @@cap@@-row tree
--- a trade that pays on AUC and fails here); and regression sampled
$|x|/3$ columns per cut, which on configuration data --- where the
signal concentrates in a few options --- mostly sampled noise.

Both were one-line fixes, and cheap iteration made the test trivial:
a scratch harness crossed cap, column sampling, leaf size and fold
policy over a random 18 sets in minutes. Two popular suspects were
acquitted on the spot --- leaf size 20 was catastrophic (+45\% error:
the deep leaves are load-bearing) and 5x5 folds changed nothing (the
cross-validation policy was never the artifact). Feeding regression
@@rcap@@ rows and every column was the whole story. Mean
$\mathit{rel}$ fell from 0.57 to @@all_b30@@ over all @@nAll@@ sets and from 0.43 to
@@cfg_b30@@ on {\tt config}, where the inversion is gone
(@@cfg_b1@@ for one tree against @@cfg_b30@@ for thirty); over all
@@nAll@@ sets the thirty-tree step now cuts @@gain@@\% off a single
tree and wins on @@beat@@.

One suspect stays open for the next draft: a {\tt balsa} leaf
predicts its {\em mean} and {\tt predict} {\em averages} across
trees; both steps minimise {\em squared} error, yet we score {\em
absolute} error, whose minimiser is the median. Re-scoring on RMSE
--- or predicting medians --- costs little and is not yet run, and
\S\ref{sec:prompts} is about saying which is which.

\paragraph{The data set still dominates the learner.} Table~\ref{tab:regvar}
applies the decomposition of Table~\ref{box:variance} to $\mathit{rel}$.
Over the @@nRan@@ sets it covers, the data set owns @@dA@@\% of the
variance against the learner's @@mA@@\%, a ratio of @@ratioA@@ to 1, with
@@fA@@\% to the luck of the fold and @@oA@@\% to interaction.
Table~\ref{tab:clsvar}, the same split on AUC over the same three
learners, reads @@dA@@ against @@mA@@ as well. Two corpora and two
losses, and the answer does not move: {\em which data set you are on
matters far more than which of these learners you run.}

Group by group the two tables also fail in the same place. Each has one
group where the learner catches up --- {\em static} in
Table~\ref{tab:clsvar} at 40 against 36, {\em large, mixed} and {\tt
config} here at about two to one --- and in both corpora those are the
groups where one learner is simply built for the job (TabICL on the
small static-analysis sets, LightGBM on the big configuration ones).
Everywhere else the data set wins by an order of magnitude or more. That
is the finding this paper keeps returning to, and it is the reason a
cheap, legible baseline is worth building before an expensive one.

